# AutoSD — example environment configuration
#
# HOW VITE ENV VARS WORK
# ----------------------
# Only variables prefixed with VITE_ are embedded into the client bundle by
# Vite (via `import.meta.env`). Everything else stays server-side/CLI-only.
#
# *** SECURITY RULE ***
# Anything prefixed with VITE_ is PUBLIC. Anyone who can load the app can read
# it in the shipped JavaScript. NEVER put secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords)
# in a VITE_ variable. See SECURITY.md and docs/SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE.md.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` for local development and adjust values.
# `.env` is git-ignored; `.env.example` is the committed template.

# --- Runtime mode -----------------------------------------------------------
# development | production | test (usually set by the CLI: `vite build` => production)
VITE_MODE=development

# --- Logging ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum log level rendered to the console: debug | info | warn | error
# In production builds the default is "warn" unless overridden here.
VITE_LOG_LEVEL=debug

# --- Embedding provider -----------------------------------------------------
# mock   — deterministic offline provider (default, no network, no key needed)
# local  — local transformers model (downloads weights on first use)
# openai — OpenAI-compatible embeddings (see the THREE MODES below)
VITE_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=mock

# --- Corpus -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Directory scanned by the corpus watcher during development.
VITE_CORPUS_DIR=corpus

# =============================================================================
# AI / EMBEDDINGS — pick exactly ONE of three modes
# =============================================================================
# The resolved mode is exposed as config.openaiMode ("none" |
# "browser-endpoint" | "server-side"). Details:
# docs/SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE.md §3.8.
#
# ── MODE 1: NO EXTERNAL AI (default) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave VITE_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=mock (or "local") and set nothing below.
# Fully offline; no endpoint, no key. This is what CI and the demo use.
#
# ── MODE 2: SAFE BROWSER ENDPOINT (PUBLIC variable) ─────────────────────────
# Point VITE_OPENAI_BASE_URL at YOUR OWN gateway that accepts requests WITHOUT
# a client-side secret (IP allowlist, signed short-lived URLs issued by your
# infra, or an internal keyless proxy). AutoSD sends NO Authorization header.
#
#   VITE_OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://gateway.example.com/v1     # ← PUBLIC (bundled!)
#   VITE_OPENAI_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small                # ← PUBLIC
#
# Validation rules enforced by src/app/config.ts (violations fall back to the
# official endpoint + a name-only warning):
#   - must be an absolute http(s) URL;
#   - https REQUIRED in production builds (http tolerated in dev/test);
#   - credential-bearing query params (api_key/token/secret/…) → rejected;
#   - embedded `sk-…` key material anywhere in the URL → rejected;
#   - userinfo (`user:pass@host`) is stripped and flagged;
#   - api.openai.com itself NEVER counts as a browser endpoint (it always
#     requires a secret, which must never live in a browser).
#
# ── MODE 3: SERVER-SIDE PROVIDER (SECRET variable) ──────────────────────────
# Keep OPENAI_API_KEY in the process environment of your own server/CLI only.
# The browser never sees it; point clients at your server endpoint instead
# (e.g. a same-origin /api/embeddings passthrough that injects the key
# server-side). In static browser builds no process env exists, so this mode
# resolves to Mode 1 (mock) there automatically.
#
#   export OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."     # shell / CI secret store — NOT this file

# Non-secret model id used by modes 2 and 3 (safe to expose).
VITE_OPENAI_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# =============================================================================
# SECRETS — server-side / CLI only. NEVER prefix with VITE_. NEVER commit.
# =============================================================================
# OPENAI_API_KEY is read ONLY from the process environment (never from
# import.meta.env), so it can never be bundled into client code. When absent,
# AutoSD automatically falls back to the Mock embedding provider.
#
# Do NOT add real keys here. If you need per-machine secrets use `.env.local`
# (git-ignored) with a NON-VITE_ name, or inject them via your process manager.
#
# NOTE: if you accidentally define a VITE_-prefixed variable whose name looks
# like a secret (e.g. VITE_OPENAI_API_KEY), config.ts records a warning — the
# value would be public to anyone loading the app. Fix it immediately.
